  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 248 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Following the passage of decaying front today, \nthe old boundary will be positioned just to our south tomorrow \nas a wave of low pressure develops. Meanwhile, another cold \nfront to our northwest will be approaching. The combination of \nlarge scale lift ahead of this front and the moisture advection \nfrom the system south of the region appears to help blossom rain\nover our region during the afternoon, generally expanding \ntowards the north and east with time. Model spread continues to \nbe large with regards to the exact footprint of the rain, still \nfavoring our southern Vermont locales while rain chances become \nmore questionable as you go northward. Increasing low level \nmoisture will support some instability, but most guidance shows \ninsufficient lapse rates to get deep convection and associated \nthunder. Still, slight chances of thunder statistically are \nwarranted at this time over southern/eastern portions of the \nregion during the late afternoon into the early evening before \nshowers exit to our east.\n\nFriday continues to look like a classic early fall day. \nFollowing a secondary cold front passing through early in the \nday, much drier, cool air will be evident. Have trended winds \nand wind gusts higher as guidance has consistently shown a gusty\nnorthwest flow pattern that would allow widespread 30 MPH gusts\nin much of northern Vermont, with slightly lighter winds in \nnorthern New York. There will be impressively dry air aloft to \ntap into such dew points will plummet during the morning as the \nboundary layer deepens. As a result, low humidity values can be \nexpected in lower elevations especially, likely below 40% in the\nChamplain Valley and Connecticut River Valley.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Following a breezy afternoon, cool high pressure\nto our northwest may crest sufficiently overhead that winds \nslacken enough to allow for frost development, especially in \nmost sheltered locations above 1000 feet elevation. The latest \nNBM 10th percentile low temperatures seems like a good first \nguess if radiational cooling is as efficient as it could be in \nthis type of setup, showing temperatures cooling into the low to\nmid 30s throughout the Adirondacks and central and eastern \nVermont Saturday morning. Model output statistics for perennial \ncold spot at the Adirondack Regional Airport currently shows a \nlow of 29, which is only a couple degrees lower than what was \nobserved yesterday morning and seems reasonable. \n\nWith light winds and a cooler start to the day, high \ntemperatures Saturday will probably be near to slightly lower \nthan on Friday. The air will remain crisp with dew points again \nmainly in the 30s during the day. Precipitable moisture will be \nquite low, as the ensemble mean values only gradually increase \nabove 50% of normal from west to east as the air mass slightly \nmodifies.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Still quite a bit of uncertainty with regards to\npossibility of some rain showers Saturday night into Sunday. \nECMWF has continued to keep our region drier with system \nremaining south of our region. GFS has now returned to a \nsolution with our region having some light rain Saturday night \nand Sunday. Will hope for better model agreement as we get \ncloser to the weekend. For now have chance for showers mentioned\nfrom 06-18z Sunday. Weather pattern will remain active through \nnext week with additional chances for showers on Tuesday and \nthen again towards the end of the work week. Temperatures will \nstart out cooler than normal this weekend then trend back \ntowards seasonal normals by mid week.",
